73388671875, -0.00439453125, -0.01953125, -0.01035308837890625, 0.0120849609375, 0.00937652587890625, 0.013092041015625, 0.039703369140625, -0.00829315185546875, 0.005680084228515625, -0.0030765533447265625, 0.0194854736328125, 0.026336669921875, 0.049713134765625, 0.004596710205078125, 0.007434844970703125, -0.030181884765625, -0.029998779296875, 0.025909423828125, -0.011871337890625, 0.0228424072265625, 0.0020313262939453125, -0.042877197265625, -0.019134521484375, 0.01143646240234375, 0.00439453125, 0.00933074951171875, 0.028411865234375, -0.0075531005859375, 0.00022113323211669922, 0.0005626678466796875, 0.013092041015625, 0.007442474365234375, -0.0030670166015625, 0.0030269622802734375, -0.0164947509765625, -0.0167083740234375, 0.0131988525390625, -0.0198211669921875, 0.00533294677734375, 0.006359100341796875, -0.005130767822265625, 0.041961669921875, -0.0200958251953125, -0.0181884765625, 0.01126861572265625, 0.010406494140625, 0.021453857421875, -0.04937744140625, 0.0003046989440917969, 0.0021877288818359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and when human direction of the task remains. In this article, we clarify two key forms of human direction. **Specified delegation** refers to human input provided before AI execution, such as instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task. **Iterative coproduction** refers to human interventions during production, where users correct or redirect provisional AI outputs as the task unfolds.\n\nWe focus on these forms of delegation using data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI provides aggregate monthly data on human-AI interactions, distinguishing between two modes of use: **1P API**, which captures direct traffic through Anthropic\u2019s application programming interface (typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows), and **Claude.ai**, which aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces (more commonly used by individual professionals and general users). We selected this data source and time frame because the AEI offers granular, mode-specific usage data during a period of stable platform features, allowing for a focused analysis of delegation patterns. While this limits generalizability to other platforms or periods, it provides a clear window into current practices.\n\nOur analysis tests two hypotheses: (1) whether a stronger **work-related orientation**\u2014measured as the proportion of interactions flagged as work-related versus personal\u2014correlates with more specified delegation within each mode, and (2) whether the increase in iterative coproduction is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. **Work-related orientation** is operationalized as the percentage of monthly activity classified as work-related by AEI\u2019s tagging system. **Specified delegation** is measured by the presence of explicit instructions or constraints in user prompts, while **iterative coproduction** is measured by the frequency of user interventions (edits, follow-up prompts) during a session. An **eligible record** is a node-month pair with sufficient data in both modes and clear classification of use type.\n\nTo estimate effects, we simulate a reallocation of ten percentage points from personal to work-related use within each node-month pair, using level-0 O*NET tasks as the analytic unit. The main analysis is restricted to 1,411 node-month pairs that are observed and eligible in both modes, ensuring comparability. This restriction, while reducing the sample size, increases internal validity by focusing on cases with complete data, though it may introduce bias if these pairs are not representative of all users.\n\nResults show that specified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) when work-related orientation rises. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). These effect sizes indicate that work-related use is associated with more explicit up-front human direction, especially in API contexts, while iterative engagement is more pronounced in the Claude.ai interface. The confidence intervals suggest these differences are statistically robust, though the practical magnitude is moderate.\n\nIn summary, our findings suggest that the mode of human-AI interaction shapes when and how human direction is exerted, with implications for the governance of generative AI. Specified delegation is more prevalent in structured, work-oriented API use, while iterative coproduction is more common in interactive, user-facing platforms. This highlights the need for governance frameworks that account not only for the amount of AI execution but also for the timing and visibility of human input. Future research should examine whether these patterns hold across other platforms and time periods, and how they relate to broader debates on accountability and oversight in AI deployment."}}